title One ratchet that held two debts
prompt The baton -- take the next agent-doable lap in the language lane, reds first.
rota lap 4129, row 4 -- Earth, the row that breathes in. Threshold, the clock and the mark, Two Rooms, the marked value. The row asks what already stands true before anyone argues with it, and its lesson landed twice this lap: a story I had about my own scan (that its population was a proxy for two rooms) did not survive the measurement, and a number I had shipped the lap before turned out to be two numbers.
think Opened with the cold roster while reading the thread. One red stood, reds_spine_derive, and I traced it before touching anything else.
think The trace: construction/REDS.md bound %530 to 20260907.000030 while construction/archive/REDS-a-lock-is-shared-by-design-a-pen-never-is-rows-530.md bound %530 to 20260906.225150. Both were on the anointed spine, so the derived-spine law's repair -- renumber the unshared row -- had no target.
think git merge-base --is-ancestor settled the order. 3717dabca, which published the shelf's %530, is an ancestor of 0cbb8457b, which published mine on the same number, so the earlier arrival keeps it and the later one moves. I made that move, and then withdrew it -- see the obs below. What I keep is the measurement.
think Then the lane. Last lap I booked %532 -- the desk witness names its 218 desks by hand while glow/gen/ holds 352 -- and landed glow_desk_reach to measure the gap at uncovered=129. This lap I read the third enumeration that row named and did not measure: the sample-permission case in tools/g/glow_run_worker.sh.
think The measurement is exact and it split the ratchet. The witness passes zero arguments to every desk it names, 218 run lines all ending .glow"]. The worker refuses an argument for any stem off its list of 94, of which 46 live in glow/gen/. All 46 are uncovered and not one is covered.
obs I withdrew the spine repair rather than land it, and the reason is a peer rather than a doubt. Between my measurement and my rebase the state moved twice: a peer folded my row off the pin onto its own shelf, and another booked the collision as REDS 20260907.014654, OPEN, saying plainly it is not theirs to take because both rows belong to other ships and renumbering either breaks citations a lap cannot count. That is a peer escalating a question to Keaton, and answering it over their head is not mine to do. What IS mine is the half they could not reach: the later row is my own, so I could count its citations. Three, all inside construction/archive -- a shelf title, a row header, a recital line -- and zero anywhere else. The card carries that number beside their row. The move is three lines and waits on the word.

obs The withdrawal cost me the repair and bought the right thing. My renumber was made against a tree where my row stood on the living pin at %530 with one citation; by the time I rebased, that tree no longer existed. Had I insisted, I would have resolved a peer's open escalation with a state they never saw, and left them to discover it from a diff.

obs A story of mine died on contact with the ground, which is what the earth row is for. Forty-eight of the worker's 94 stems name no desk under glow/gen/, and my first reading was that my own scan's population was a proxy -- one room standing in for the tree's Glow corpus. The measurement refused it: those 48 live in src/gate and src/shape, and those rooms are covered by their own guards at 47 of 49 and 39 of 39. The scan's scope was right and my worry was not.
obs The split is what the sum could not say. Eighty-three uncovered desks cost one three-line block apiece. Forty-six cost a judgment first -- what argument proves this gate? -- and then a fourth hand-written enumeration to hold the answers, which is the shape %532 booked in the first place. Worse, the sum could stand still through a real fault: cover one bare desk on the lap a sample-taking desk lands, and 129 is unchanged while the room has grown a hole.
obs A boundary that clean is never an oversight. Zero overlap between 46 permitted and 218 covered is the instrument's shape drawn in full, and it sat there for a lap because nobody crossed the two lists.
obs My own error, found by the first case that called it. The control's runscan helper shifted once and handed the remaining words -- the output path included -- to env, so env read the output file as its first assignment and the scan never ran. It had been dead code since I wrote it, called by nothing, and every field it produced read empty. A helper nobody calls is a helper nobody has checked.
obs A second of mine, in the same file's door. The scan recited 213 covered and 134 uncovered -- the numbers a mis-collated comm produced before LC_ALL=C was exported. The code was fixed and the prose was not. A door reciting a number its own body disproves is this meter's own subject, one room in.
obs The QA reading below B was a setting mismatch rather than a quality fault. The new REDS shelf read 68 at Field and 100 at Meter, which is the class a ledger shelf belongs to; a peer shelf reads 100 the same way. Measure at molt time, and closing a frame with a number is a legitimate answer.
